---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="MeshCore Paths"
  description="A path server for MeshCore: it maps the repeater backbone from the packets it hears, and answers direct messages with the best path between two nodes."
  page="about"
>
  <h1>Ask the mesh for a path.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A MeshCore path server listens to the mesh, works out which repeaters can hear
    which, and answers a direct message with the best direct path from you to
    anyone. You stop flooding the whole network to reach one person.
  </p>

  <div class="stats" id="stats" aria-live="polite">
    <div class="stat"><b data-k="nodes">–</b><span>repeaters mapped</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b data-k="links">–</b><span>links published</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b data-k="observers">–</b><span>observers</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b data-k="generated">–</b><span>last update</span></div>
  </div>
  <p><a href="/explore/">Explore the map and look up a route →</a> <span class="badge" id="sample" hidden>sample data</span></p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">Send a direct message to a path server node:</p>
    <p class="mono">path Example Ridge</p>
    <p>It answers with the hops to use, or tells you to flood when it isn't confident:</p>
    <p class="mono">#path1 5f3c19e0b7a2 w=1 4e,a1,73 c=0.97</p>
    <p class="mono" style="margin-bottom:0">#path1 5f3c19e0b7a2 flood</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>1. Listen</h3>
      <p>Each flood packet carries the hash of every repeater that relayed it. A packet that
      arrives via <span class="mono">A → B → C</span> proves B hears A and C hears B.
      The observer keeps only the path and the adverts. Message contents stay encrypted
      and are thrown away.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>2. Work out who's who</h3>
      <p>Most repeaters still use a 1-byte hash, so dozens share each value. The resolver
      rules candidates out (wrong type, too far apart, not heard directly, gone quiet). It
      then weighs the rest against links already known to be real, and gives every link a
      confidence score.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>3. Answer only when sure</h3>
      <p>Routes use only high-confidence links. They avoid hops where a second repeater with
      the same hash would also forward the packet. They use 2- or 3-byte hashes when every
      repeater on the way supports them. Otherwise the answer is "flood".</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Built to grow</h2>
  <pre class="diagram">observer (radio + computer) ── signed batches ──▶ ingest ──▶ observation store
                                                                │ every ~10 min
                                                                ▼
                                                            resolver
                                                                │
                              graph-&lt;hash&gt;.json (immutable) + latest.json
                                                                │ CDN
                    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┐
              this site (route lookup runs in your browser)     every path server's answers</pre>
  <p>Visitors only ever load static files from the CDN. Graph files are named by their
  hash, so they never change and are cached forever. Route lookup runs in your
  browser. More observers mean a bigger, more accurate map without more load on any one
  machine: each radio only sees the routes that reach it, so merging what several
  radios hear fills in the gaps.</p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Never stored or published: message contents (DMs, channels, rooms), who sent what, or when.</li>
    <li>Published: repeaters and rooms (name, key prefix, advertised location) and the links
    between them. Repeaters already broadcast all of this to everyone in range.</li>
    <li>Observers are never named, and their own direct links are left out, so the map does not
    reveal where an observer sits.</li>
    <li>Phones and handheld companions never appear.</li>
  </ul>

  <script>
    import { loadGraph } from '../lib/data.ts';
    loadGraph().then((g) => {
      const set = (k: string, v: string) => {
        const el = document.querySelector(`#stats [data-k="${k}"]`);
        if (el) el.textContent = v;
      };
      set('nodes', g.nodes.length.toLocaleString());
      set('links', g.links.length.toLocaleString());
      set('observers', g.sample ? '–' : String(g.stats.observers));
      const mins = Math.round((Date.now() - Date.parse(g.generated)) / 60000);
      set('generated', mins < 60 ? `${Math.max(mins, 0)} min ago` : mins < 2880 ? `${Math.round(mins / 60)} h ago` : `${Math.round(mins / 1440)} days ago`);
      if (g.sample) document.getElementById('sample')!.hidden = false;
    }).catch(() => {
      const el = document.querySelector('#stats [data-k="generated"]');
      if (el) el.textContent = 'unavailable';
    });
  </script>
</Base>
